## Slot vocabulary ({{slot-count}} slots inherited or declared)

AI Actor memorials inherit `:mm/Memory` (and `actor`) plus AI-specific slots:

- **`model-family`** (required) — e.g., `claude` / `codex` / `gpt`
- **`version`** (required) — e.g., `opus-4-7-1m` / `cli-0-130-0`
- **`runs-in-context`** (required) — rel-paths to context memorials
- **`belongs-to`** — organization rel-paths (provider company)
- **`supersedes`** — predecessor version rel-paths
- **`peer-of`** — sibling actor rel-paths at same release
- **`context-window`** — numeric token capacity
- **`capabilities`** — list of named capability tags

{{slot-table}}

## Canonical body structure

```markdown
## What this AI actor is

<Model family, version, key distinguishing capabilities.>

## Context window + capabilities

<Token capacity, distinguishing capabilities, notable tools or
behaviors.>

## Provenance

<Provider organization, release date, version-chain context.>

## Version chain

<What this supersedes; what supersedes it (if any).>

## Operating contexts

<Which contexts this actor operates in (cross-reference context
memorials).>

## See also

<Sibling actors, parent organization, governing rules.>
```

## Composition with adjacent classes

- **`organization`** — AI actors typically `belongs-to` the provider organization
- **`context`** — AI actors run in specific contexts (sandboxed vs unsandboxed)
- **`rule`** — rules may scope to specific AI actor versions
- **`event`** — AI actors are typical triggers for `pre-tool-use`, `pre-grounding`, `user-prompt-submit` events

